meta {
  name: Reset Password (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/reset-password
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "token": "{{passwordResetToken}}",
    "password": "{{password}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("signs the user in with a working token pair", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("accessToken");
    expect(body).to.have.property("refreshToken");
    expect(body.user).to.have.property("permissions");
  });

  test("does not reactivate — isActive is untouched", function () {
    // A reset is not a reactivation. The user was already active here, so this
    // asserts the value survived rather than being set as a side effect.
    expect(res.getBody().user).to.not.have.property("deactivatedAt");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /auth/reset-password

  Public (`@Public()`, `auth: none`). Set a new password from a reset link and
  sign in.

  **Body**
  | field    | type   | rules                          |
  |----------|--------|--------------------------------|
  | token    | string | non-empty; the raw reset token |
  | password | string | non-empty, min length 8        |

  **Response `201`** — the same `{ accessToken, refreshToken, user }` shape as
  `POST /auth/login`, so the page lands the user in the app already signed in
  rather than bouncing them to `/login` to retype what they just chose.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — password under 8 characters.
  - `401` — unknown, expired or already-used token, or the user has since been
    deactivated. Not distinguished.

  Carries the tightest public `@Throttle` in the API
  (`PASSWORD_RESET_SUBMIT_RATE_LIMIT`, default 5/min): an unauthenticated write
  that changes a credential.

  ## What completing a reset does
  - Sets the password (bcrypt, 12 rounds).
  - **Burns the token** — single use, cleared whether or not it had expired.
  - Clears any pending invite token, keeping one live credential per account.
  - **Ends every existing session for that user**, by bumping `tokenVersion`.
    Access tokens fail their next request and the refresh token cannot mint a
    replacement. Without that, a password change would leave an attacker's token
    working until it expired.
  - Leaves `isActive` alone. Resetting must never reactivate a removed employee.

  ## Why this request is safe to re-run
  It resets the demo producer back to `{{password}}` — the seeded
  `ChangeMe123!` — so the tenant's logins are unchanged after a full
  `npx @usebruno/cli run`, the same way **Reactivate User** undoes
  **Deactivate User**.

  > ⚠ It does sign that user out everywhere. If you have the web app open as
  > `producer@demoagency.local` while running the collection, that tab's next
  > request 401s and you will need to sign in again — with the same password.
  > This runs last in the last folder precisely so nothing downstream is holding
  > a producer token when it happens.
}
